The scan identified six open TCP Ports.

The exposed services indicated that the target was running remote access, web RPC and SMB-related services.

These services were selected for further investigation.

5. WEB SERVER VULNERABILTY SCANNING - NIKTO

Objective

Investigate the web server running on the target and identify potentially insecure configurations and known vulnerability signatures.

Command : nikto -h 10.0.2.6

Explanation:

nikto launches the Nikto web server scanner.

-h specifies the target host.

10.0.2.6 is the target IP address.

Explanation:

Nikto launches the nikto web server scanner

-h specifies the target host 

10.0.2.6 is the target IP address.

Nikto was used to examine the HTTP service running on port 80.

Scan Information:

Component                                    Result

Nikto Version                                2.6.1

Target IP                                   10.0.2.6

Target Port                                   80

Platform                                  Linux/Unix

Web Server                                Apache 1.3.20

SSL Module                                 mod_ssl 2.8.4

OpenSSL                                       0.9.6b


Findings:

5.1 Potential Inode Information Disclosure

Nikto reported that the server may leak inode information through ETags.

The finding referenced:

CVE-2003-1418

This could expose additional file metadata through HTTP responses.


5.2 Outdated Apache Version

Nikto identified several vulnerability signatures associated with the legacy Apache installation.

The reported checks included:

Apache 1.x versions associated with potential denial-of-service and code-execution vulnerabilities.

Apache 1.3 versions below 1.3.27 associated with potential denial-of-service issues.

Apache 1.3 versions below 1.3.29 associated with potential buffer overflow issues involving mod_rewrite and mod_cgi.

These are scanner-reported matches and require independent validation.


5.3 mod_ssl Vulnerability Signature

Nikto identified the following SSL module:

mod_ssl/2.8.4

The scanner reported a potential remote buffer overflow vulnerability affecting mod_ssl versions below 2.8.7.

The reported impact may include remote shell access.

Further investigation is required to establish whether the target is actually vulnerable.


5.4 HTTP TRACE Method Enabled

Nikto identified the following allowed HTTP methods:

GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, TRACE
The HTTP TRACE method was enabled on the server.

TRACE can create unnecessary security exposure and should generally be disabled unless required.

5.5 CGI Directory Check

Nikto reported:

No CGI Directories found

This means the scan did not identify CGI directories through its checks.

6. Summary of Findings

The reconnaissance and scanning activities identified the following:

The Kioptrix target was discovered at 10.0.2.6.

The target exposed six open TCP ports.

SSH was accessible on port 22.

HTTP was accessible on port 80.

RPCBind was accessible on port 111.

NetBIOS-SSN was accessible on port 139.

HTTPS was accessible on port 443.

Port 32768 was identified as FileNet-TMS by Nmap.

Apache 1.3.20 was identified by Nikto.

Potential inode information disclosure was reported.

Several legacy Apache vulnerability signatures were identified.

A potential mod_ssl vulnerability was reported.

The HTTP TRACE method was enabled.

